---
title: "Full Row Editing"
---

Configure the grid to enter and exit edit mode for an entire row at once.

## When to Use Full Row Editing

Full row editing is particularly useful when:

- **Form-like Editing**: Your data represents complete records (like user profiles, orders, or inventory items) where editing multiple fields together makes sense.
- **Related Fields**: Changes to one field often require updates to related fields in the same row.
- **User Experience**: You want to provide a clear visual indication that a record is being edited.
- **Data Validation**: You need to validate relationships between fields before saving (e.g., ensuring a start date is before an end date).

## Enable Full Row Editing

To enable full row editing, set the grid option `editType = 'fullRow'`.

```{% frameworkTransform=true spaceBetweenProperties=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    editType: 'fullRow'
}
```

## How It Works

When full row editing is enabled, the editing behavior changes:

1. **Starting Edit**: When you start editing any cell, all editable cells in that row become editable simultaneously.
2. **Navigation**: Press {% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%} to move between cells within the editing row. Read-only cells remain focusable but not editable.
3. **Stopping Edit**: Pressing {% kbd "↵ Enter" /%} or clicking outside the row commits all changes and stops editing for the entire row.
4. **Cancelling Edit**: Press {% kbd "⎋ Esc" /%} to discard all changes and exit edit mode for the row.

## Events

When a row stops editing:
- `cellValueChanged` event fires for each column that changed
- `rowValueChanged` event fires once for the entire row
- `rowEditingStarted` fires when edit mode begins
- `rowEditingStopped` fires when edit mode ends

## Custom Cell Editors

If using custom cell editors, the cell editors will work in the exact same way with the following additions:

-   `focusIn`: If your cell editor has a `focusIn()` method, it will get called when the user tabs into the cell. This should be used to put the focus on the particular item to be focused, e.g. the `textfield` within your cell editor.
-   `focusOut`: If your cell editor has a `focusOut()` method, it will get called when the user tabs out of the cell. There is no intended use for this; it's just there to complement the `focusIn()` method.

## Integration with Other Features

### Validation

Full row editing works seamlessly with [Cell Editing Validation](./cell-editing-validation/). You can validate individual cells as well as perform cross-field validation using `getFullRowEditValidationErrors`. The row will only save if all cells pass validation.

### Undo / Redo

When [Undo / Redo](./undo-redo-edits/) is enabled, undoing a full row edit will revert all cells in the row to their previous values as a single operation.

### Read-Only Cells

Cells with `editable: false` remain read-only during full row editing, but they can still receive focus when tabbing through the row.

### Keyboard Navigation

Use {% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%} and {% kbd "⇧ Shift" /%}+{% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%} to navigate between editable cells within the row. Cells with `suppressNavigable: true` will be skipped during tab navigation.

## Popup Editors

Full row editing is not compatible with popup editors. This is because a) the grid would look confusing to pop up an editor for each cell in the row at the same time and b) the complexity of navigation and popup is almost impossible to model, so the grid and your application code would be messy and very error prone. If you are using full row edit, then you are prevented from using popup editors.

This does not mean that you cannot show a popup from your 'in cell' editor - you are free to do that - however the responsibility of showing and hiding the popup belongs with your editor. You may want to use the grid's focus events to hide the popups when the user tabs or clicks out of the cell.

## Starting Edit Programmatically

You can start full row editing programmatically using the grid API:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
// Start editing a specific cell - the entire row becomes editable
api.startEditingCell({
    rowIndex: 2,
    colKey: 'athlete'
});
```

Even though you specify a single cell, the entire row enters edit mode when using `editType: 'fullRow'`.

## Example: Full Row Editing

The example below demonstrates full row editing. In addition to standard full row editing, the following should also be noted:

-   The 'Price' column has a custom editor demonstrating how you should implement the `focusIn()` method.
    Both `focusIn()` and `focusOut()` for this editor are logged to the console. Note that `focusIn()`
    and `focusOut()` are only called when the user is tabbing between cells when editing, they are not called
    as the user double clicks on a cell to start editing that cell, or the user finishes editing that cell by
    e.g. hitting the {% kbd "↵ Enter" /%} key.
-   Pressing {% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%} / {% kbd "⇧ Shift" /%} & {% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%} while editing will move the focus between the cells on the editing row. Read only cells will be focusable while the row is in edit mode.
-   The 'Suppress Navigable' column is not navigable using {% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%} / {% kbd "⇧ Shift" /%} & {% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%}. In other words, when tabbing around the grid, you cannot tab onto this cell.
-   The **Read Only** column is not editable, so when the row goes into edit mode, the cell in the **Read Only** column cannot be edited.
-   The button will start editing line two. It uses the API to start editing a cell, however the result is that the whole row will become editable starting with the specified cell.
-   `cellValueChanged` and `rowValueChanged` events are logged to console.

